Related: #112760 (closed as not planned).

[steipete’s closing comment](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/112760#issuecomment-6380197979) says current main resolved the original list-absence symptom and an explicit access-revoked signal is an optional follow-up, requiring a concrete current UI failure for a fresh issue. This PR proposes that optional contract; whether to accept it remains a maintainer decision.

## What Problem This Solves

This PR proposes an explicit Control UI signal when a sharing change changes a viewer’s access to an open session. A missing row in a filtered session list is not an access decision.

## User Impact

The open composer immediately becomes unavailable after access is revoked or changes to read-only, and recovers when shared access returns. Suggest mode continues to allow a suggestion draft until access is revoked. Missing list rows remain neutral. No configuration or data migration is required.

## Why This Change Was Made

Publish a connection-specific `session.access.changed` event after a committed visibility or membership change. The closed payload contains only `agentId`, `sessionKey`, `sessionId`, and `access` (`revoked`, `read-only`, or `read-write`). Publication uses the canonical sharing target and current recipient authority; no-op, denied, stale, and unrelated-recipient changes do not publish an access fact.

Control UI stores restrictions for the admitted connection and exact session instance. A restoration retires the matching restriction; existing session-row and scope checks still decide whether an action is allowed. Composer, pending typing, Talk, read-marker, and dashboard-default writers consume that state. Disconnect or connection replacement retires it. This is additive: older clients may ignore the event; native composer behavior, authorization policy, persistent schema, and protocol version are unchanged.

## Evidence

Validated source tree `5642f750a7ef18405f663dbf09cf6121b02ff880` after resolving the upstream conflicts. The [immutable current proof packet](https://github.com/zhangguiping-xydt/openclaw/blob/2bdd4ef8b535160e89d54254910ef56a2565ea98/README.md) identifies the exact candidate, eight production UI captures, observed event payloads and original receipt hashes. All 12 public files were independently fetched into an empty repository and matched their hashes.

- **Source checks:** 189 distinct scoped tests passed (24 protocol, 11 Gateway, 154 UI), along with formatting of all 31 changed paths, type-aware lint of 30 TypeScript paths, all 29 affected core/test TypeScript graphs, separate UI production types, protocol checks and both line ratchets. After completing the normal plugin dependency closure, type-aware lint, the Gateway owner tests and both type checks were freshly repeated; the other same-candidate checks retain their original execution identity. No regression coverage or ratchet allowance was removed or increased.
- **Normal builds:** scripts-enabled frozen installation for the 128 selected workspace dependency closure passed, followed by normal `corepack pnpm --silent openclaw --help` and `corepack pnpm ui:build`. Source manifests and lockfile stayed unchanged. No skip-script flag, handwritten build stamp or manual dependency link substituted for installation.
- **Real Gateway and production browser:** visibility and membership changes, redundant mutations and denied writes were exercised through real RPCs. The browser stayed on one admitted connection and exact session instance through seven access events: read-only, restored, revoked, recovered, suggest, withdrawn and restored. Eight inspected 1280×960 frames and DOM observations show the corresponding composer state; the draft survived withdrawal/restoration. No chat or suggestion was submitted.
- **Lifecycle:** current native runtime validation `1793439058311` passed with verified import provenance. Both browsers and all owned children joined; proxy/model sockets and port claims were released. After authenticated status identified the owned Gateway PID/start, normal SIGTERM produced a clean shutdown and CLI exit 0. No SIGKILL or forced cleanup was used. Earlier setup/timeout failures remain separate failed records.

Read-only restriction:

![Read-only composer](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zhangguiping-xydt/openclaw/2bdd4ef8b535160e89d54254910ef56a2565ea98/02-read-only-disabled.png)

Shared access restored, with the suggestion draft retained:

![Restored composer with retained draft](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/zhangguiping-xydt/openclaw/2bdd4ef8b535160e89d54254910ef56a2565ea98/08-shared-final-restored.png)

**Proof boundary and risk.** This is real-Gateway/production-browser proof with synthetic trusted-proxy identities and a metadata-only loopback fixture; zero model requests occurred. It does not prove an external identity provider or model inference. Native Swift/Android behavior and the public users.merge/users.setRole revoke/retire/reconnect lifecycle were not rerun; older packets remain historical evidence. The current-head final-effect transport proof below now covers three deterministic identity/scope interleaves at publication. No new migration or Doctor claim is made.

The optional notification contract and visibility-filter exception remain maintainer decisions described in [the review](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/179661#issuecomment-6346166861). The closed access fact is an observation, not authority: recipient identity/scope/session checks remain at delivery, and restoration removes only the matching transient restriction without overriding existing row or scope decisions. No persistent schema, configuration format or protocol version change is proposed.


### Current-head final-effect authority proof (2026-11-06)

**Passed all three scenarios on unchanged head `40267a5b05246c960ea6f059346871304a15fb52`, tree `5642f750a7ef18405f663dbf09cf6121b02ff880`.** After native recipient preparation and the real SQLite sharing commit, the driver changes the target recipient's identity, withdraws its write scope, or withdraws all event-reading scopes, then invokes the saved native publication callback. Each case records **zero target transport sends and zero received access frames**, while an unaffected recipient receives the committed change. The target transport stays open, an ordered wire marker is received, and restoring the same connection's authority restores normal delivery.

This exercises the real sharing handler, connection owner, resident profile preparation, projection, broadcaster, `createGatewayWebSocketTransport`/codec, and real WebSocket server/client pairs. Send observers forward all original arguments; they do not filter frames. Each case also proves normal delivery before the interleave and naturally closes all six socket endpoints plus the listener and state owner afterward. Exit 0; 3 tests passed, Vitest 164.68s (mostly compilation/transformation). Linux, Node 24.22.2, pinned pnpm 12.15.0; complete scripts-enabled frozen installation; execution in a network-none, secretless container. The production source, manifests and lockfile remained unchanged.

| Interleave immediately before saved publication | Target sends / received frames | Unaffected sends / received frames | Same-connection restored sends / received frames |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Distinct synthetic resident identity, canonical recipient-profile preparation | 0 / 0 | 1 / 1 | 1 / 1 |
| `[operator.read, operator.write]` → `[operator.read]` | 0 / 0 | 1 / 1 | 1 / 1 |
| `[operator.read, operator.write]` → `[]` | 0 / 0 | 1 / 1 | 1 / 1 |

**Proof boundary.** This supplemental driver uses an internal deterministic scheduling seam and synthetic admitted-client identity/scope state. It supplements the existing exact-tree normal Gateway/browser packet; it does not claim a new authenticated RPC or browser run. Public `users.merge` and `users.setRole` retire/revoke connections instead of offering arbitrary in-place identity/scope mutation, and that public lifecycle is not reproduced here. Unrelated Gateway services, OAuth and clipboard integrations use standard test scaffolding; the sharing, recipient authority, broadcast decision and actual transport are retained. The write-scope case preserves read access and tests invalidation of prepared authority; the empty-scope case directly tests loss of event-reading authority. Maintainer acceptance of the optional event contract and independent merge/security gates remain separate.

An independent Codex reviewer inspected the complete 31-file diff from `ed4e2ec14308c380eb286d0a553cab396d1d4ebd` to the current head, the final driver, all 72 ordered receipts, original runtime output, source/driver hashes and joined cleanup. No actionable introduced code finding; the scoped body-only evidence update passed review. This is not a claim that autoreview ran or that a maintainer approved the feature.

