Related: #112760 (closed as not planned).

[steipete’s closing comment](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/112760#issuecomment-6380197979) says current main resolved the original list-absence symptom and an explicit access-revoked signal is an optional follow-up, requiring a concrete current UI failure for a fresh issue. This PR proposes that optional contract; whether to accept it remains a maintainer decision.

## What Problem This Solves

This PR proposes an explicit Control UI signal when a sharing change changes a viewer’s access to an open session. A missing row in a filtered session list is not an access decision.

## User Impact

The open composer immediately becomes unavailable after access is revoked or changes to read-only, and recovers when shared access returns. Suggest mode continues to allow a suggestion draft until access is revoked. Missing list rows remain neutral. No configuration or data migration is required.

## Why This Change Was Made

Publish a connection-specific `session.access.changed` event after a committed visibility or membership change. The closed payload contains only `agentId`, `sessionKey`, `sessionId`, and `access` (`revoked`, `read-only`, or `read-write`). Publication uses the canonical sharing target and current recipient authority; no-op, denied, stale, and unrelated-recipient changes do not publish an access fact.

Control UI stores restrictions for the admitted connection and exact session instance. A restoration retires the matching restriction; existing session-row and scope checks still decide whether an action is allowed. Composer, pending typing, Talk, read-marker, and dashboard-default writers consume that state. Disconnect or connection replacement retires it. This is additive: older clients may ignore the event; native composer behavior, authorization policy, persistent schema, and protocol version are unchanged.

## Evidence

Validated source tree `5642f750a7ef18405f663dbf09cf6121b02ff880` after resolving the upstream conflicts. The [immutable current proof packet](https://github.com/zhangguiping-xydt/openclaw/blob/2bdd4ef8b535160e89d54254910ef56a2565ea98/README.md) identifies the exact candidate, eight production UI captures, observed event payloads and original receipt hashes. All 12 public files were independently fetched into an empty repository and matched their hashes.

- **Source checks:** 189 distinct scoped tests passed (24 protocol, 11 Gateway, 154 UI), along with formatting of all 31 changed paths, type-aware lint of 30 TypeScript paths, all 29 affected core/test TypeScript graphs, separate UI production types, protocol checks and both line ratchets. After completing the normal plugin dependency closure, type-aware lint, the Gateway owner tests and both type checks were freshly repeated; the other same-candidate checks retain their original execution identity. No regression coverage or ratchet allowance was removed or increased.
- **Normal builds:** scripts-enabled frozen installation for the 128 selected workspace dependency closure passed, followed by normal `corepack pnpm --silent openclaw --help` and `corepack pnpm ui:build`. Source manifests and lockfile stayed unchanged. No skip-script flag, handwritten build stamp or manual dependency link substituted for installation.
- **Real Gateway and production browser:** visibility and membership changes, redundant mutations and denied writes were exercised through real RPCs. The browser stayed on one admitted connection and exact session instance through seven access events: read-only, restored, revoked, recovered, suggest, withdrawn and restored. Eight inspected 1280×960 frames and DOM observations show the corresponding composer state; the draft survived withdrawal/restoration. No chat or suggestion was submitted.
- **Lifecycle:** current native runtime validation `1793439058311` passed with verified import provenance. Both browsers and all owned children joined; proxy/model sockets and port claims were released. After authenticated status identified the owned Gateway PID/start, normal SIGTERM produced a clean shutdown and CLI exit 0. No SIGKILL or forced cleanup was used. Earlier setup/timeout failures remain separate failed records.

Read-only restriction:

